    "turn_text": "Yeah. Of course now, and I'll answer it as you probably would expect people in my line of work to start by answering it. I don't like to deal with hypotheticals. But that being said, I do think that there is a unified body in this Congress that is supportive of President Trump We understand the Constitution. Nobody is blindly following anybody. But I do think that we understand that there was a mandate given by the people of America. Everybody likes the Democrats love to argue and they hate that word mandate. But the reality is that the U.S. Senate was flipped to Republican. The U.S. House majority was maintained even as thin as it is. And the White House was turned back over to President Donald Trump to be the 47th President of the United States. There was a mandate across this country. And I believe that everybody in this Congress recognizes that. That was a beauty of last Tuesday nights, a budget resolution that passed the House. It blew a hole in the framework and the narrative of the left because suddenly they could no longer say Republicans can't govern, they can't get together. Even the Senate started their own version of what they were going to do because I'm not sure they had confidence and faith that we could get a majority of vote and pass something in the U.S. House. And yet we did it last Tuesday night. That was a big victory for the president. That was a big victory for Speaker Johnson. It was a big victory for Congress showing that we can do this. And so an answer to your hypothetical. Um, if it's going to require strong action, um, even against, uh, out of whack judge that is taking place when the president stands up and says, no, we can't go there, I think that this Congress has his back at this point. That's me personally from what I can gather from this Congress. There's a United front right now.",
